      <section class="case" aria-labelledby="case-title">
        <h2 id="case-title">What the corpus held, and what it does not say</h2>

        <p class="orientation">Five counted states, each held at a fixed commit.</p>

        <ol class="moments">
          {
            MEASUREMENTS.map((measurement) => (
              <li class="moment">{measurement.phrase}</li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>

      </section>

      <section class="figure" aria-labelledby="figure-title">
          <div class="dev-rate">
<div class="wrap">

<h2 id="figure-title">MWE development rate</h2>
<p class="sub">Written and published activity, each indexed to its own 2025-10 value on a
logarithmic axis, with recorded AI token use plotted separately below on its own token
scale over the same months. The three are separate measures of separate things and are
never summed.
Series end 2026-07-31; nothing after that date appears anywhere on this page.</p>

<p class="sub"><strong>&ldquo;Rate&rdquo; here means quantity per month, not rate of
change.</strong> Each point is that month&rsquo;s output measured against 2025-10, so a
rising line means a larger month relative to the base &mdash; not a faster increase.
Month-over-month growth is computed nowhere on this page, and it would not look like this:
the written index peaks in 2026-06, while the largest month-over-month rise is 2026-03.</p>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><div class="k">Words written</div><div class="v">1,680,982</div>
    <div class="n">1,660 documents, deduplicated</div></div>
  <div class="tile"><div class="k">Posts published</div><div class="v">192</div>
    <div class="n">Medium, by its own publication dates</div></div>
  <div class="tile"><div class="k">AI tokens, logged period</div><div class="v">1,815,940,311</div>
    <div class="n">logging begins 2026-05-24; plotted below</div></div>
</div>

<h3>Indexed shape only: written against published</h3>
<p class="lede">Each line is change relative to its own 2025-10 value. Vertical separation
is therefore divergence in <em>relative change</em> - it is a real quantity, and it is not a
difference in output: absolute levels and original units, words against posts, are not
comparable at all. Where the lines cross depends on the baseline chosen, so the crossing
point carries no meaning on its own.</p>
