<p class="note">Every plotted point carries its value, and the same figures appear in the
table below. The eight earlier months are shaded rather than drawn at zero, because they are
not zeros - nothing was logged for them, which is a weaker statement than nothing happened.
The monthly table marks them <em>not recorded</em> for the same reason.</p>

<h3>The three series separate in the record</h3>
<div class="note">
<ul>
<li><strong>Written and published move in opposite directions.</strong> Writing spikes to
663.7 in 2025-11 and falls back below its 2025-10 level twice before 2026-03; from
2026-03 it stays above that level and reaches an index of 2,332.8 in 2026-06,
23.3 times the base. <em>2026-03 is where the rise becomes sustained, not where the
highest earlier month sits</em> - both readings are in the line and only the second is a
trend. Publishing declines from that same base to a single post in 2026-06, after which the
export boundary makes the record silent rather than empty.</li>
<li><strong>The documents got longer as well as more frequent.</strong> Median document
length stays below 500 words through 2026-02 and runs above 1,200 from 2026-05, so
the rise is not only a rise in file count.</li>
<li class="flag"><strong>Timing overlap, not causal evidence.</strong> The writing curve
turns at 2026-03. The first recorded AI tokens are 2026-05-24, 2 months later, and
the bulk is 2026-07. The turn therefore precedes the tool use rather than following it,
which is a fact about ordering and still not a fact about cause. The token series is
evidence about tool use, not about effort, quality, or output.</li>
</ul>
</div>

