<h3>What changed in this rebuild</h3>
<div class="note">
<p>The previous version dated every document by file modification time. That records when a
file was last organised, not when it was written. The corpus carries an ISO week tag in its
text, written at the time of writing, and that is the better source. This rebuild applies
the three-tier rule settled 2026-07-29: week tag first; otherwise a modification time that
does not fall inside a known bulk filesystem operation; otherwise the document is dropped
as undatable.</p>

<p class="flag"><strong>One ordering defect was found and corrected.</strong> The earlier
implementation applied the bulk-operation filter <em>before</em> reading the week tag. That
filter exists to reject unreliable modification times, but a tagged document does not
depend on its modification time at all, so the filter was discarding dates it had no reason
to distrust. Measured on the current corpus, 772 of 1,677 tagged files, 46 percent, were
being thrown away that way. Reading the tag first recovers 384 of them into the
deduplicated set and raises the total from 1,517,560 words to 1,680,982.</p>

<table>
<caption>Months where the ordering change matters. Everything else is identical between
the two columns.</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Filter first</th><th>Tag first</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">2025-11</th><td>99,948</td><td>161,338</td><td>+61%</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">2025-12</th><td>8,588</td><td>48,177</td><td>+461%</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">2026-01</th><td>7,927</td><td>14,547</td><td>+84%</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">2026-02</th><td>2,711</td><td>6,427</td><td>+137%</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">2026-03</th><td>48,378</td><td>56,061</td><td>+16%</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">2026-04</th><td>58,422</td><td>69,653</td><td>+19%</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">2026-05</th><td>344,620</td><td>371,369</td><td>+8%</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">2026-06</th><td>560,647</td><td>567,091</td><td>+1%</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>

<h3>What the numbers rest on</h3>
<div class="note">
<ul>
<li class="flag"><strong>835 documents are dated by their own week tag;
830 fall back to modification time.</strong> The fallback is the weaker half and it
carries the most recent months: no document dated 2026-07 or later carries a tag. Repairing
that is deferred, so the right-hand end of the written line rests on the weaker source and
should be read with less confidence than the left.
<br><br>Those two figures count a larger set than the 1,660 documents above, and the
difference is accounted for rather than left open: 835 + 830 = 1,665
documents carry a usable date; 4 of them fall after the 2026-07-31 cut-off,
leaving 1,661 inside it; 1 is dated before 2025-09, where this table
begins. 1,661 less 1 is the 1,660 shown here.</li>
<li><strong>Every headline figure is the table's own column, added up.</strong> The tiles
are sums over the months shown, not over a wider window that happens to be close. Anything
the rebuild dated outside 2025-09 to 2026-07-31 is out of both.</li>
<li><strong>The published series stops where its export was taken, not where publishing
stopped.</strong> The Medium index is a 2026-07-07 export, so a post published after that
date could not appear in it. 2026-07 reads <em>not in export</em> in the table and on the
chart for that reason. Earlier zeros are measured zeros; this one is not.</li>
<li><strong>The token panel is a record of what was logged, not of what was used.</strong>
It begins where logging begins. A month with no tokens means nothing was recorded for that
month, which is a weaker statement than nothing happened.</li>
<li><strong>Exclusions are inherited, not chosen here.</strong> One-time extractions, the
narrative interface layer, and registry and process output are removed under rules settled
2026-07-29.</li>
<li><strong>Indexing is per series, and the token panel is not indexed at all.</strong>
Written and published are each indexed to their own 2025-10 value; tokens are plotted as
tokens. Nothing on this page may be compared across panels by vertical height.</li>
<li><strong>None of the three series is a productivity measure.</strong> Words written,
posts published, and tokens spent are counts of artefacts and of machine work. They do not
measure quality, difficulty, or how much of the work was thinking rather than typing.</li>
</ul>
</div>

</div>
